  THE THEOREM, hypotheses stated exactly.
    Setting.  h = h_base + eta(r)[delta_10 - B] with h_base = -E rho^2/3 + (rho-independent), delta_10 =
      -E rho^2 G/3, r = -rho E/3, and the trace kinetic factor A = 1 + eta G.  E > 0 and G, B depend only on
      (xi, u) -- NOT on rho -- for any pressure of the form f(Xtilde), since Xtilde is fixed by (xi, u).  The
      reduced scalar UV kinetic coefficient is a_UV = E A/6 + h,rhorho/4.
    Lemma (S1, S2, derived not assumed).  a_UV = -(1/b) d/dr[b(r)^2 eta'(r)],  b = E(G r^2 + B E/3)/12.
    THEOREM.  Fix (xi, u) with G != 0.  Let eta be C^2 and non-constant on a transition interval [r1, r2] with
      eta'(r1) = eta'(r2) = 0.  Then a_UV < 0 on a subset of positive measure of (r1, r2), for EVERY B in R.
    Proof.  b is a quadratic in r with at most one positive root r0.  Put beta = r0 if r0 lies in (r1, r2),
      else beta = r2.  On (r1, beta) b has one strict sign, and integrating the Lemma against the weight b,
      int_{r1}^{beta} b a_UV dr = -[b^2 eta']_{r1}^{beta} = 0, because eta'(r1) = 0 and either b(beta) = 0 or
      eta'(r2) = 0.  If a_UV >= 0 there, then b a_UV has one sign and integrates to zero, so a_UV = 0 on
      (r1, beta), hence b^2 eta' is constant = its value 0 at r1, hence eta is constant there; repeating on
      (beta, r2) (where eta'(beta) = 0 by continuity) makes eta constant on all of [r1, r2].  Contradiction. []
    COROLLARY (pressure-blind).  At any interior r* with eta''(r*) = 0 -- one exists, since eta' vanishes at
      both ends and not in between -- a_UV(r*) = -(E G r*/3) eta'(r*), which contains no B at all.  The IC5
      switch has a RISING branch (r^2 in (1/2, 3/4)) and a FALLING branch (r^2 in (5/4, 3/2)) with opposite
      sign(eta'), while sign(G) is a single number at fixed (xi, u); so one of the two branches is ghostly
      whatever f is chosen.
    ALONG A TRAJECTORY, where E, G, B vary with r, the same conclusion follows with the weight
      mu = exp(int a/b)/b, since mu b = exp(int a/b) > 0 kills the boundary term and sign(mu) = sign(b); the
      extra hypothesis needed is that b does not vanish on an open subinterval.  R2b tests exactly this case
      numerically, on the model's own solved trajectories.
    WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED.  Nothing about Hamiltonian redesigns that break the Lemma's setting -- in particular
      a correction whose second rho-derivative does NOT cancel the E A/6 term, so that the plateaus stop being
      marginal.  That is the door this lane leaves open, and it is where IC13/IC14 should push.

PART 4 -- what a pressure CAN do: the sliver the lead's run actually samples
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Near the eta = 1 edge, eta' and eta'' are both exponentially small but eta'' dominates (the mollifier leaves
  the plateau with zero slope), so a_UV -> -b eta'' and sign(a_UV) = sign(b).  The lead's 501 samples never
  leave that sliver (eta stays above 0.9957).  There the curing condition is one inequality, b > 0, i.e.
        G r^2 + B E/3 > 0,      G = 1/J - 1 = e^{2w+1/6} - 1,   B = e^S f(S),
  and it can be met in TWO ways, which is the practical content of this lane:
    ROUTE A (background, free):  G > 0  <=>  2w + 1/6 > 0  <=>  (1-u) xi < 1/12.  No pressure needed at all.
    ROUTE B (pressure, costly):  where G < 0,  e^S f(S) > 3 |G| r^2 / E.
    IC12's run takes NEITHER: its (1-u) xi crosses 1/12 at n = 207, and from there Route B would need
    B between 0.000143031 and 0.0848034 while its f supplies 2.3044e-7 -- short by 368006.0x.
  [PASS] R1 [cure] in the sampled sliver the sign of a_UV is exactly the sign of b, so the curing condition is the explicit inequality  G r^2 + e^S f(S) E/3 > 0   (holds at every sample; on Route B, IC12's f falls short by 3.68e+5x)
    the geometric form of Route A, checked: (1-u) xi = 1/12 exactly where the ghost starts -- at n = 206 it is 0.0832845717455, at n = 207 it is 0.0833613739764, and 1/12 = 0.0833333333333
  [PASS] R1b [cure] the ghost onset in IC12's own run is EXACTLY the crossing of the geometric condition (1-u) xi = 1/12, not anything about its pressure   (the a_UV sign change (n = 207) and the (1-u)xi = 1/12 crossing (n = 207) are the same step)

  R2: exhibit an explicit f that MEETS the inequality, re-solve the roots with it, and re-run the test.
  Each candidate f gets its OWN admissible plateau window (IC11 chose S = .2 for its own f by exactly this
  predicate: 3/4 <= r^2 <= 5/4 with PX > 0, Q_clock > 0, 0 < cs^2 <= 1, rho > 0, H_physical > 0), so no
