PART 5 -- pricing the ghost: where it is for a real Solar System and a real galaxy, and its rate
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  r = -N p/(3 m h0) is the trace of the METRIC MOMENTUM in units of h0 -- the local expansion trace, not a
  radius.  eta = 1 for r^2 in [3/4, 5/4] (cosmological), eta = 0 for r^2 <= 1/2 and r^2 >= 3/2 (static).  So:
    * a STATIC system has p = 0 identically, hence r = 0, hence eta = 0 on a whole neighbourhood, with every
      first jet vanishing (IC5's static plateau).  The Solar System and a virialised disc carry NO ghost.
    * the ghost region is where the local expansion trace passes between 0.707 and 0.866 of its cosmological
      value: the boundary between the Hubble flow and the collapsed region -- the TURNAROUND shell.
  [PASS] P1 [physics] the Solar System and the galactic disc are exactly ghost-free: p = 0 gives r = 0 and eta = 0 with all jets vanishing, so a_UV = 0 there and the static sector is untouched   (the ghost cannot be placed in the disc; it is a shell at the edge of the collapsed region)

  spherical infall, stated: theta/(3H) = 1 - f delta/3 with f = Om^0.55 = 0.530; delta(<r) from the
  enclosed mass.  rho_m = 2.688e-27 kg/m^3 (H0 = 67.4, Om = 0.315).  Both a0 footings enter only through
  the h0 calibration below -- the shell location is a RATIO statement and is footing-independent.
                    system     M (Msun)   outer edge (Mpc)   inner edge (Mpc)   thickness (Mpc)
     Solar System (static)            -                    no shell: p = 0, eta = 0 identically
                 Milky Way     1.50e+12              1.724              1.503             0.222
            galaxy cluster     1.00e+15             15.065             13.126             1.939
  [PASS] P2 [physics] the ghost shell of a real galaxy sits at the turnaround scale, NOT in the disc: 1.50-1.72 Mpc for the Milky Way (0.22 Mpc thick), 13.1-15.1 Mpc for a cluster   (spherical-infall estimate with the assumptions printed above; every bound system carries one such shell)

  rate.  a_UV < 0 is a WRONG-SIGN KINETIC coefficient, so the growth rate is not bounded by the background:
  omega^2 = -(gradient/|a_UV|) k^2 gives Gamma = c_eff k, growing linearly in k up to the EFT cutoff.
  Taking c_eff ~ c (the reduced scalar's gradient coefficient is O(1) in the same units), the SLOWEST mode
  that fits in the Milky Way's shell (lambda = 0.22 Mpc) already grows at
  Gamma = 2.751e-13 s^-1, an e-folding time of 1.152e+05 yr = 0.1152 Myr;
  every shorter mode is faster without bound.  |a_UV| itself reaches 7.04064 in
  units of the model's own h0^2 by the end of the lead's run -- an O(1) wrong-sign coefficient, not a whisker.
  EXTERNAL BENCHMARK, not computed here: a Lorentz-violating ghost coupled to gravity decays the vacuum into
  ghost + ordinary pairs; the diffuse gamma-ray background forces its cutoff below ~3 MeV (Cline, Jeon &
  Moore 2004).  That is the standard price of a ghost in a finite region, and it is not softened by the
  region being finite: the shell is spatially extended and permanent.
  [PASS] P3 [physics] the ghost is not benign in its shell: the slowest mode that fits grows in 0.115 Myr, and the rate is UNBOUNDED in k (cut off only by the EFT cutoff)   (a negative kinetic coefficient is a UV problem; a finite region does not regulate it)

  a0 calibration of the model's own rate unit h0 (both footings), stated conversions:
    the model fixes a0^2 = 27 e^{-1/2}/(8 ell^2) and Lambda = 6 e^{-1/2} - a0^2 U(4/9) in units m = h0 = 1,
    kappa = 6 h0^2.  a0 is a RATE there (it sits beside Lambda), so a0_phys = c * a0_model * h0 and
    3 H_Lambda^2 = Lambda gives H_Lambda = sqrt(Lambda/3) h0.  Hence a0_phys/(c H_Lambda) is a pure number
    the model PREDICTS, independent of h0.
